    "excerpt": "How do you preserve typographic nuance across four distinct media brands while sharing a unified codebase? The secret lies in decoupling semantic tokens from raw CSS values.",
    "content": "\n      <p class=\"article-lead\">Design systems frequently suffer from token explosion. Within eighteen months, well-intentioned teams amass 600 color tokens and 40 arbitrary font sizes, paralyzing developers and confusing designers.</p>\n\n      <h2>The Three-Tier Token Architecture</h2>\n      <p>To establish durable typographic harmony, we organize our tokens into three inviolable layers:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Tier 1: Global Primitives</strong> — Raw scales: <code>--font-size-100: 0.75rem</code>, <code>--font-size-400: 1rem</code>, <code>--ratio: 1.25</code>.</li>\n        <li><strong>Tier 2: Semantic Intent</strong> — Functional roles: <code>--type-editorial-heading-lg</code>, <code>--type-body-default</code>.</li>\n        <li><strong>Tier 3: Component Context</strong> — Scoped implementations: <code>--card-title-size</code>, <code>--hero-lead-leading</code>.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <blockquote>\n        \"Tokens are the contracts between aesthetic vision and computational execution. Without clean tiers, design systems decay into chaos.\"\n      </blockquote>\n\n      <h2>Decoupling Brands from Hardcoded Values</h2>\n      <p>When a new sister publication launches, developers do not rewrite component styles. They simply swap the semantic token tier, transforming a high-density tech journal into a luxury serif publication overnight without touching a single HTML template.</p>\n    "
