    "excerpt": "Setting background to #000000 and text to #FFFFFF is the most common typographic crime on the modern web. Here is the optical science behind comfortable night reading.",
    "content": "\n      <p class=\"article-lead\">When Apple popularized system-wide Dark Mode, millions of web developers hastily declared <code>bg-black text-white</code> and called it accessibility. In reality, pure white text against pitch black causes visual halation—a glowing fringe around glyphs that exhausts the ciliary muscles.</p>\n\n      <h2>The Warm Charcoal Sanctuary</h2>\n      <p>In our publication’s Night Edition, we calibrated a tripartite darkness matrix:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Base Paper Surface:</strong> <code>#0c0b0a</code> (Warm Stone 950) instead of cold <code>#000000</code>.</li>\n        <li><strong>Body Glyph Tint:</strong> <code>#d4d4d8</code> (Zinc 300) offering an optimal 11.2:1 contrast ratio rather than a searing 21:1.</li>\n        <li><strong>De-weighted Strokes:</strong> Serifs appear visually bolder on dark backgrounds due to photon scatter; reducing the font-weight variable by 40 units restores daytime optical weight.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Circadian Typography Shifts</h2>\n      <p>By monitoring ambient light sensors via modern browser APIs, our layout can subtly warm color temperature past 9:00 PM, filtering blue wavelengths and easing cognitive transition toward natural sleep.</p>\n    "
