    "excerpt": "Move beyond uniform 12-column frameworks. Discover how CSS Subgrid, auto-fit repeat tracks, and asymmetric editorial spans create intentional tension and organic rhythm.",
    "content": "\n      <p class=\"article-lead\">For more than a decade, web design was imprisoned by the tyranny of the symmetric 12-column grid. Frameworks made layout easy, but turned every website into an indistinguishable collage of three-column card rows.</p>\n\n      <h2>The Promise and Power of CSS Subgrid</h2>\n      <p>Until recently, CSS Grid cards could not share row alignments with their neighbor cards. If card A possessed a 3-line title while card B had a 1-line title, their footers drifted hopelessly out of alignment.</p>\n\n      <pre><code class=\"language-css\">/* Establishing subgrid alignment across article cards */\n.article-grid {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(320px, 1fr));\n  gap: 2rem;\n}\n\n.article-card {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-rows: subgrid;\n  grid-row: span 4;\n}</code></pre>\n\n      <h2>Designing for Intentional Editorial Tension</h2>\n      <p>Asymmetric spans allow key investigative dispatches to occupy two columns while secondary briefs stack vertically, creating the rhythmic visual pacing that makes physical magazines so compelling.</p>\n    "
